>>>markdown
# AdventureWorks

Microsoft's AdventureWorks Power BI sample, translated from its semantic model into Malloy.

Reseller and internet sales for a fictional bicycle manufacturer: 121,253 order lines
across 397 products, 18,485 customers and 702 resellers, over three fiscal years.

`sales` is the fact source. Everything else is a dimension reachable from it.

## What changed in translation

The Power BI file carried four **auto date/time tables** (`LocalDateTable_*` and a
`DateTableTemplate_*`), one generated per date column. They are an artifact of a setting
rather than a modeling decision, so they are gone; `dates` is the real date dimension.

Power BI declared **three relationships from `sales` to the date table** and kept two of
them inactive, reaching them through a measure that called `USERELATIONSHIP`. Malloy has
no notion of an inactive join, so all three are named instead: `order_date`, `due_date`
and `ship_date`. The Power BI measure `Sales Amount by Due Date` is therefore not a
measure here at all, just `total_sales` grouped along `due_date`.

The 4-row table Power BI called `Table` is a category sort order, joined from `products`.
It is `category_sort` here.

Column names were snake_cased (`Sales Amount` to `sales_amount`, `State-Province` to
`state_province`).

>>>malloy
import "adventureworks.malloy"

# bar_chart
run: sales -> {
  group_by: products.category
  aggregate: total_sales
  order_by: total_sales desc
}

>>>markdown
## Sales by territory and channel

>>>malloy
run: sales -> {
  group_by: territories.group
  aggregate: total_sales, order_line_count
  nest: by_channel is {
    group_by: channel
    aggregate: total_sales
    order_by: total_sales desc
  }
  order_by: total_sales desc
}

>>>markdown
## The two date paths

Ordered versus due, the same dollars distributed differently. This is what the Power BI
model expressed with an inactive relationship and `USERELATIONSHIP`.

>>>malloy
run: sales -> {
  group_by: fiscal_year is order_date.fiscal_year
  aggregate: sales_by_order_date is total_sales
  order_by: fiscal_year
}

>>>malloy
// Same dollars, allocated along the due date instead. Both columns total
// $109,809,274.20; only the distribution across years moves.
run: sales -> {
  group_by: fiscal_year is due_date.fiscal_year
  aggregate: sales_by_due_date is total_sales
  order_by: fiscal_year
}

>>>markdown
## Product margin

>>>malloy
run: sales -> {
  group_by: products.category, products.subcategory
  aggregate: total_sales, gross_profit, margin_pct
  order_by: gross_profit desc
  limit: 15
}
